Protocol and design detail: Modport Expressions (2009). Use: When the same interface is used with different signal subsets or derived signals; check tool support for exact syntax. Benefit: Fewer duplicate interface definitions; clearer connection contract. From MODULE5 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

32
00:07:58,082 --> 00:08:22,874
Protocol and design detail: Virtual Interface (2009). Design: Rarely used in RTL; RTL typically connects to a concrete interface instance. Verification: Common in UVM and other OOP testbenches; allows one driver class to work with any instance of the interface. From MODULE5 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

33
00:08:23,224 --> 00:08:51,472
Protocol and design detail: Integer Types and Sizes. byte, shortint, int, longint: 8, 16, 32, 64 bits; signed by default. byte unsigned, int unsigned: Unsigned variants. Clarifications in 2012: Representation and conversion rules; use in RTL for constants and parameters. From MODULE5 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

34
00:08:51,822 --> 00:09:23,262
Protocol and design detail: Operators Added or Clarified. inside: Already in 2005; 2009/2012 clarify set membership and use with ranges. Wildcard equality ==?: X/Z in the right-hand side act as “don’t care” in comparisons. Set membership and case (inside): Used in case statements and constraints; semantics clarified in 2012. From MODULE5 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

35
00:09:23,612 --> 00:09:54,644
Protocol and design detail: Array Methods (2012). Array methods: .size(), .delete(), .sort(), .reverse(), etc. (exact set is revision-dependent). Queues: .push_back(), .pop_front(), .insert(), etc. Use in RTL: Limited (arrays in RTL are often fixed); methods are common in testbenches and checkers. From MODULE5 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

36
00:09:54,994 --> 00:10:24,514
Protocol and design detail: Array Assignment and Ordering. Packed vs unpacked: Assignment rules and ordering (row-major, etc.) are clarified. Array copy: Whole-array assignment and copying; compatibility of dimensions. Use in RTL: Multi-dimensional arrays (e.g. memories) and parameterized dimensions; follow tool-supported subset for synthesis. From MODULE5 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

37
00:10:24,864 --> 00:10:52,824
Protocol and design detail: String Methods. Strings: string type; methods such as .len(), .substr(), .atoi(), etc. (as defined in 2012). Use in RTL: Rare (strings are mainly for messages and file I/O in testbenches). Use in testbenches: Logging, report generation, file names. From MODULE5 Topics Covered — protocol/design depth.

38
